Two consecutive FuncType stack args don't corrupt adjacent args
===============================================================

Pins the bug from `feedback_sysl_trisc_two_fnptr_then_scalar.md`.

TRISC `evalAndPushArg` (and the matching TCall regArg pre-eval)
used to **leak** the 16-byte source descriptor temp allocated by
`TFuncRef` rather than rewinding it before pushing the
destination pair. With two adjacent FuncType args plus a scalar
neighbour, the leaks interleaved between adjacent destination
slots in the callee's stack-arg region, shifting every
subsequent arg off its expected offset. The callee then read
`g` from `f`'s leaked temp and `x` from `g`'s leaked temp —
both 16B in the wrong place.

The fix gates the rewind on `arg.typ.isInstanceOf[FuncType]`
specifically (env_ptr never points into the temp), keeping the
existing leak for `InterfaceType` (where inline iface
construction stores `data_ptr` into the same temp block —
rewinding would reclaim the struct before the callee
dereferences `data_ptr`).

Helper fns in this file exercise every shape the original repro
hit: two fnptrs first then int, scalar first, fnptr-int-fnptr
sandwich, and the indirect call (`g(f(x))`) over distinct fns
so the bug can't hide behind the symmetry of identical args.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    incr(x: int) -> int = x + 1

    dbl(x: int) -> int = x * 2

Two fnptrs then scalar
----------------------

The exact failing signature from the memo. Three params, first
in r1 by TRISC ABI, second and third on the stack. `g` (second
fnptr, 16 bytes) and `x` (scalar, 8 bytes) must land at
contiguous fp+24 / fp+40 slots in the callee.

    take_two_fnptr_then_int(f: (int) -> int, g: (int) -> int, x: int) -> int
        g(f(x))

    #test
    test_two_fnptr_then_int_distinct_fns() -> unit
        // distinct fns prove `g` isn't being read from `f`'s leaked
        // temp — that would silently give incr(incr(5)) = 7 by chance.
        val r = take_two_fnptr_then_int(incr, dbl, 5)
        // (5+1)*2 = 12
        assert(r == 12, s"expected 12 = dbl(incr(5)), got $r")

    #test
    test_two_fnptr_then_int_symmetric() -> unit
        // also pin the symmetric case so regression catches a fix
        // that only happens to work on distinct fns.
        val r = take_two_fnptr_then_int(incr, incr, 5)
        // incr(incr(5)) = 7
        assert(r == 7, s"expected 7 = incr(incr(5)), got $r")

Scalar first, then two fnptrs
-----------------------------

Reg arg is scalar (5), both fnptrs on the stack. The reg-arg
path no longer needs the rewind (scalar isn't an aggregate),
but the two consecutive FuncType stack-arg pushes still must
not interleave temps.

    take_int_first(x: int, f: (int) -> int, g: (int) -> int) -> int
        g(f(x))

    #test
    test_int_first_distinct_fns() -> unit
        val r = take_int_first(5, incr, dbl)
        assert(r == 12, s"expected 12, got $r")

    #test
    test_int_first_swap() -> unit
        val r = take_int_first(5, dbl, incr)
        // dbl(5)=10, then incr(10)=11
        assert(r == 11, s"expected 11, got $r")

Fnptr, int, fnptr sandwich
--------------------------

The alternating pattern that worked even before the fix. Keep
it here so regressions on the alternating layout are caught too.

    take_fnptr_int_fnptr(f: (int) -> int, x: int, g: (int) -> int) -> int
        g(f(x))

    #test
    test_fnptr_int_fnptr() -> unit
        val r = take_fnptr_int_fnptr(incr, 5, dbl)
        assert(r == 12, s"expected 12, got $r")

Compose helper — fnptrs first then int, called indirectly
---------------------------------------------------------

`compose(f, g, x) = g(f(x))` was the original repro that
panicked. Same shape as `take_two_fnptr_then_int` but the body
calls both fnptrs in sequence — exercises both the call-site
push and the in-body indirect call against adjacent FuncType
locals.

    compose(f: (int) -> int, g: (int) -> int, x: int) -> int
        g(f(x))

    #test
    test_compose_distinct() -> unit
        val r = compose(incr, dbl, 7)
        // (7+1)*2 = 16
        assert(r == 16, s"expected 16, got $r")

    #test
    test_compose_swap() -> unit
        val r = compose(dbl, incr, 7)
        // 7*2 + 1 = 15
        assert(r == 15, s"expected 15, got $r")

Three fnptrs and a scalar — stress the multi-leak case
------------------------------------------------------

Three adjacent FuncType stack args with a scalar at the end.
Without the fix, three leaks interleave with three pushed
pairs and the final scalar; the callee reads garbage for
every arg past the first.

    chain_three(f: (int) -> int, g: (int) -> int, h: (int) -> int, x: int) -> int
        h(g(f(x)))

    #test
    test_chain_three_distinct() -> unit
        val r = chain_three(incr, dbl, incr, 3)
        // ((3+1)*2)+1 = 9
        assert(r == 9, s"expected 9, got $r")
